titre$Artificial Key to the Genera of Clavarioid Homobasidiomycetes

com$Corner 1970 SUPPLEMENT TO "AMONOGRAPH OF CLAVARIA AND ALLIED GENERA" / Adapt GF mai 2012


1,2$  Spores with coloured wall, not amyloid.
1,4$  Spores white or with pinkish or yellowish contents.
2,0$  Spores fuscous, brown or purplish brown, echinulate, tuberculate, lobate or angular; hyphae usually not inflating; tissue fibrous, coriaceous or floccose, often cyanescent in potash; fruit-bodies erect with flattened branching $ Thelephora, p. 11
2,3$  Spores ochraceous to ferruginous, smooth or ornamented, not angular.
3,0$  Erect, branched; hyphae inflating $ Ramaria, p. 237
3,0$  Odontioid with decul"ved spines; hyphae of spines not inflating $ Kavinia, p. 226
4,5$  Spores with amyloid wall or ornamentation.
4,9$  Spores not amyloid.
5,0$  Spores subglobose, echinate-cristate; dimitic with skeletal hyphae; erect with flattened branching $ Amylaria, p. 15
5,6$  Spores smooth or asperulate.
6,7$  Erect.
6,8$  Hydnoid.
7,0$  Dichotomously branched; gloeocystidia absent. $ Lentaria, p. 228
7,0$  Pyxidately or dichotonously branched; gloeocystidia present $ Clavicorona, p. 143
7,0$  Small, simple; gloeocystidia absent $ Pistillaria, p. 110
8,0$  Glbeocystidia present; hyphae inflating, becoming. thick-walled; generally massive with the branches decurved as spines $ Hericium, p. 171
8,0$  Gloeocystidia absent; hyphae not or little inflated; fruit-bodies _small to minute, single, unbranched, positively geotropic, often densely gregarious $ Mucronella, p. 171
9,0$  Dimitic with colourless skeletal hyphae; fruit-bodies filiform, simple, or branched with filiform branches, erect deflexed or decumbent, tough $ Pterulaceae, p. 194
9,10$  Monomitic or dimitic with dichophyses.
10,11$  Hyphae with more or less thickening, yellow-brown walls, without clamps, not inflating; fruit-bodies with flattened branching, tough: sterigmata straight.
10,12$  Without such character (not :"Hyphae with more or less thickening, yellow-brown walls, without clamps, not inflating; fruit-bodies with flattened branching, tough: sterigmata straight").
11,0$  Setae absent; gloeocystidia and dichophyses present . · . $ Lachnocladium, p. 178
11,0$  Setae brown; gloeocystidia and dichophyses absent . · $ Clavariachaete p. 176
11,0$  Without such characters ; hyphae with dull brownish walls $ Phaeoaphelaria, p. 21
12,0$  Young basidia subacerose; hymenium usually with oleocystidia; fruit-bodies small with distinct stem and cylindric to conic or globose head, often hollow; epiphytic. $ Physalacriaceae, p. 187
12,13$  Young basidia cylindric to subclavate; without oIeocystidia.
13,0$  Sterigmata curved, 2 (or if more, then the hyphae with dark brown walls); basidia often transversely septate at maturity $ Clavulinaceae, p. 149
13,14$  Sterigmata straight.
14,15$  Hyphae not inflating; spores smooth; fruit-body rather tough.
14,16$  Hyphae inflating or, if not, then w:ith echinulate spores.
15,0$  Basidia large, sulcate at the apex from the large sterigmata; spores large, multiguttulate; clamped; with flattened branching. $ Tremellodendropsis, p. 283
15,0$  Basidia normal $ Aphelariaceae, p. 15
16,0$  Spores with one or few guttae or echinulate $ Clavariaceae, p. 23
16,17$  Spores without such guttae, smooth.
17,0$  Simple, massive to minute; hyphae of medulla without firmly thickened walls, often mucilaginous $ Clavariadelphaceae, p. 93
17,18$  Branched; hyphal walls often firmly thick'ened.
18,0$  Decurved $ Delentaria, p. 225
18,0$  Erect $ Lentaria, p. 228
18,0$  Odontioid $ Kavinia, p. 226
